# Mobile full player fixed-region contract

The full player is an outer scroll column (player region, then chips, then a height-locked pane
sheet) with an inner scroller for pane body. The player region keeps a fixed internal geometry while
chips and pane content below it change. Nothing on this screen is sticky, overlay-pinned, or shrinks
with scroll. The mini player on other screens is a separate surface.

## Required

- Only the viewer band flexes. Every other band keeps its fixed height (`height`, not only
  `minHeight`, so reserved chrome matches what renders). The viewer band flexes for real
  (`flexGrow` / `flexShrink` with `minHeight: 0`). The square's edge is `artworkSize` from
  `resolveFullPlayerLayout`, seeded from the window on the first commit
  (`resolveFullPlayerViewport`). It must not change from a later `onLayout` — a second size during
  the slide-up is a visible flash. Large text clips inside the fixed bands instead of resizing the
  square after first paint.
- The artwork is what gives way. It has caps (`FULL_PLAYER_ARTWORK_MAX_PHONE` /
  `_MAX_TABLET`) but no floor: a viewport too short for the fixed bands shrinks the square rather
  than pushing the chip strip off the bottom edge. The region is never taller than the space the
  viewport can give it, and clips (`overflow: 'hidden'`) in the worst case.
- Region and pane-sheet sizing come from `fullPlayerLayout.ts`; do not introduce ad-hoc magic heights
  in screen styles. Band styles read the same constants the math reads (`FULL_PLAYER_REGION_GAP`,
  `FULL_PLAYER_CONTROL_STACK_GAP`, `FULL_PLAYER_REGION_TOP_PADDING`,
  `FULL_PLAYER_REGION_BOTTOM_PADDING`, `FULL_PLAYER_CHIP_HEADER_HEIGHT`) so reserved and rendered
  space cannot drift.
- Episode title and channel title share one title block (`FULL_PLAYER_TITLE_BLOCK_HEIGHT`) with a
  tight internal gap. Do not split them into two bands separated by `FULL_PLAYER_REGION_GAP`.
- Band order matches web's player modal: title block, artwork, segment band, progress, transport,
  utility. Upper seams use `FULL_PLAYER_REGION_GAP` (three of them, two without the segment band).
  Progress → transport and transport → utility use the tighter `FULL_PLAYER_CONTROL_STACK_GAP` (two
  of them). Plus
  `FULL_PLAYER_REGION_TOP_PADDING` above the title and `FULL_PLAYER_REGION_BOTTOM_PADDING` below the
  utility row (the seam before the section chips). Both paddings are chrome so the bands sum to the
  region height rather than overflowing. The chip header has no hairlines.
- The remote region (`FullPlayerRemoteRegion`) keeps the same bands and heights, always reserves
  the segment band (the chapter on its first line when there is one, the "Playing on {device}"
  status line on its second, or the status line alone), and has no section chips or pane. Moving
  between the local and the remote region counts as a new item.
- Peek sizes first paint so the chip strip sits at the fold. `resolveFullPlayerLayout` reserves the
  chip strip plus the bottom safe-area inset, minus `FULL_PLAYER_FOLD_DROP` (`spacing.lg`, 16), from
  the player height. That drop lowers the chips and the pane under them; the inset that remains is
  the pane color at the bottom edge. The drop never exceeds the inset, so the strip stays on screen
  and above the home indicator when the inset is taller than the drop. Space above the chips and
  space from chips to the pane sheet are each
  **2×** `listChipRowBottomGap` (`SectionChipRow` owns one unit below; the chip slot adds the
  second). Chips sit **above** the sheet, inset to the sheet's outer border (`spacing.md`), not to
  the pane text column. Do not add a safe-area spacer or a third pane `paddingTop`. Chips scroll
  with the player. `FULL_PLAYER_CHIP_HEADER_HEIGHT` is the default-text-size floor — the strip
  reports its measured height (`chipStripHeight`) and peek follows the larger of the two, so
  accessibility text sizes cannot leak Summary copy under the chips. Measure the chip row slot only.
- **Outer `ScrollView`** owns the player → chips → sheet column (`testID="full-player-outer-scroll"`).
  Max outer scroll equals the player region height: once the sheet is fully on screen (chips at the
  top, bottom radius visible), the outer column stops. Returning to the player is an outer gesture
  on the chips / player (or iOS status-bar `scrollsToTop` on the outer scroller). There is no
  inner-to-outer scroll chaining. The outer column is **not** a `FlatList` — its children are fixed
  (player + chips + one sheet), not an unbounded row list.
- **`FullPlayerPaneSheet`** owns the viewport-derived slot (`paneSheetHeight`): the same value as
  `minHeight`, `height`, and `maxHeight`, plus `overflow: 'hidden'`. Every tab paints that same card;
  tab bodies are children and must not compute their own height or add a spacer. Long content
  cannot grow the outer column. Pass `sheetBottomInset` from `tokens.spacing.md` into layout math so
  the bottom radius sits above the home indicator.
- **Inner `FlatList`** (`testID="full-player-section-list"`) scrolls only inside the sheet.
  `scrollsToTop={false}` so status-bar tap restores the outer player. `data` is the chapter / clip /
  official-clip rows; summary and transcript live in the footer. A chip tap swaps the pane and
  scrolls the outer column to its end (`scrollToEnd`), so the chips sit at the top and the sheet
  fills the viewport. That end is `playerRegionHeight`. Reduce Motion jumps. The inner list still
  resets to its top only when the pane changes. Nesting this list under the outer `ScrollView` is
  required product
  behavior; RN's "VirtualizedLists should never be nested" yellow box is structural and expected —
  silence it only via `ignoreFullPlayerBoundedNestedListWarning()` from
  `listVirtualization.ts` (called from `FullPlayerScreen`). Do not de-virtualize chapters/clips into
  `ScrollView` + `.map()`, and do not broaden that ignore to unrelated nesting. Summary prose shows
  the full stored notes in the sheet (**about-summary-web-mobile-alignment**).
- The pane sits in a rounded, inset sheet (`themeStyles.paneSheet`) so the fold reads as a distinct
  surface. Dark uses page ink (`background.primary`); other themes use the card surface. Chips stay
  outside that sheet. Round all four corners on the single wrapper (`FullPlayerPaneSheet`). The
  sheet does not pad its children vertically. Chapter, clip, and official-clip rows run to the top
  and bottom edges; `overflow: 'hidden'` clips the first and last rows to the radius. Summary and
  transcript keep `spacing.base` above and below the scroller, the same inset as the first chapter
  title, and that inset stays put while the prose scrolls. Empty pane copy uses that same top inset
  inside the footer. A load error in the footer keeps the pane's horizontal inset (`spacing.lg`) on
  its own wrapper, so a failed tab does not change the inset of a tab that has rows.
- The segment band (`FULL_PLAYER_SEGMENT_BAND_HEIGHT`) sits **below** the artwork. It is reserved
  for an item with chapters (`itemHasChapters`, or a non-empty loaded chapter list) or a clip,
  official clip, or chapter target, the way web reserves `--media-modal-subtitle-reserve`. A chapter
  or clip name arriving mid-playback fades into a row that is already there — it never resizes the
  artwork or moves the transport. For an item with neither, the band and one region seam are
  omitted (`hasSegmentBand: false`) and the artwork takes that height.
- Duration for the scrubber is seeded from `item.item_about.duration` via
  `resolveMediaFileDurationHintSeconds` when playback starts; native `durationSeconds > 0` still
  wins. The left clock always shows the playhead; seek stays disabled only while duration is still
  `0`.
- New player controls open sheets. Do not expand controls inline inside the fixed region.
- If a new control needs persistent space, reserve a fixed-height band for it in layout math.
- Video renders inside the artwork square and letterboxes when aspect ratios differ.
- When the active chapter has an image, that image replaces the item/channel artwork in the
  viewer square (**mobile-chapter-artwork**). Chapter list rows in the pane follow the same
  section-has-images rule.
- Playhead UI lives in leaf components that read `playbackProgressStore`; the region shell
  uses `usePlaybackSession()` only.
- Pull-to-dismiss is the native-stack top-edge gesture only (`fullScreenGestureEnabled: false`).
- Neither scroller bounces or over-scrolls, and this screen has no pull-to-refresh.
- The outer column hides its vertical scroll indicator (`showsVerticalScrollIndicator={false}`).
- The pane list draws a vertical scroll indicator (`showsVerticalScrollIndicator`, and
  `persistentScrollbar` on Android) when its content is taller than the sheet. The thumb stays the
  system pill. On iOS, `indicatorStyle` is `white` on dark themes and `black` on light themes
  (`isLightUiTheme`) so the pill reads against the sheet. It still fades after the finger lifts.
- The full player has no empty-session body. When the item completes and nothing is ahead,
  now-playing clears and the **focused** full player dismisses (`onClose`). Do not dismiss while
  Make clip is authoring (`isAuthoringHold`) or while this screen is unfocused — `goBack()` would
  pop Make clip. A skip with nothing ahead must keep the current item playing so this path never
  fires from a long-press.

## Do not

- Do not insert or remove fixed-region rows while an item plays. Content that comes and goes gets a
  band reserved for the whole item; anything else jumps the layout while a listener is watching it.
  The segment band is decided per item (chapters or a segment target), not per chapter.
- Do not let pane loading, empty states, or chip switching push transport or utility rows.
- Do not bind region size to transcript/chapter/clip body height.
- Do not pin chips, overlay the player above the list, shrink the player with scroll, or add a
  condensed now-playing bar on this screen. Do not put chips in a sticky `SectionList` header.
- Do not let tab bodies add their own `minHeight` spacer or grow the outer column — the
  **wrapper** owns the viewport-derived minHeight/height slot.
- Do not pad the pane sheet vertically to keep the first or last row off the rounded corners.
- Do not give the region a minimum height that can exceed the viewport, and do not reserve peek from
  a hardcoded chip height alone — both push the chip strip off-screen on short viewports or at large
  text sizes.
- Do not resize the artwork square from a band `onLayout` after the first commit.
- Do not subscribe the shell to progress ticks (`usePlayback()` / `usePlaybackProgress()`).
- Do not enable a full-screen vertical dismiss gesture or list bounce on the full player.
